1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,000
You don't remember being born. You don't remember your first word, your first step, or the first time someone held you and you looked up at their face. You

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:17,000
don't remember learning what a dog was, or the first time you tasted something sweet, or the moment you figured out that your hands belonged to you. And

3
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:26,000
here's the thing, those moments happened. They were real. You were there. You were conscious. You were feeling things, experiencing things,

4
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:35,000
processing an entirely new world at an astonishing rate, and then it just vanished. Everything before roughly age

5
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:44,000
three is gone for most people. Some people have a fragment or two from age four, a birthday, a car ride, a smell, but the first years of your life, the

6
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:56,000
most dramatic period of growth and learning your brain will ever undergo, exist in almost complete silence. No memories, nothing. So where did they go?

7
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:04,000
Because this isn't like forgetting where you put your keys. This is a systematic near total eraser of the first chapter

8
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:12,000
of your existence. And for a long time, nobody could satisfactory explain why it happens. Now, we have some answers, and

9
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:19,000
they are genuinely fascinating. Let's start with the obvious question. Were you even conscious back then? Were you

10
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:25,000
actually experiencing anything worth remembering? Yes. Unambiguously, yes.

11
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:32,000
Infants are not passive receivers of sensory input. They are active, curious, emotionally responsive beings from

12
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:40,000
extremely early on. Newborns can recognize their mother's voice within hours of birth. They heard it in the womb, and the memory of that sound is

13
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:49,000
already encoded before they've even seen daylight. Within days, they show preferences. They respond differently to faces they've seen versus faces they

14
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:57,000
haven't. They cry differently for hunger versus pain versus loneliness. There is someone home. A real experience in

15
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:02,000
consciousness is present from the very beginning. And yet, none of it sticks.

16
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:11,000
not in a form you can access. Now, the first major clue to understanding why comes from looking at what memory actually is and how it gets built.

17
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:19,000
Memory isn't a recording. This is probably the most important thing to understand about how the brain stores experience and it's wildly

18
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:27,000
counterintuitive. When something happens to you, your brain doesn't hit a record button and file away a video clip. What

19
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:31,000
it does is far messier and far more interesting.

20
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:39,000
Different aspects of an experience get processed by different brain regions simultaneously. The emotional content goes one place.

21
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:41,000
The sensory details go somewhere else.

22
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:47,000
The sequence of events, the narrative of what happened goes somewhere else entirely.

23
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:56,000
For you to have a retrievable autobiographical memory, a memory of the I was there and this happened to me variety, all of those

24
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:03,000
pieces need to be linked together and consolidated into a stable, accessible package.

25
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:08,000
That process requires a specific brain structure called the hippocampus.

26
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:15,000
The hippocampus is the brain's memory indexer. It's the region that binds the scattered pieces of an experience into a

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:23,000
coherent hole and files it somewhere you can later retrieve. Without hippocample involvement, you might process an

28
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:31,000
experience. You might even be affected by it emotionally, but you won't have a conscious narrative memory of it that you can deliberately recall. And here's

29
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:39,000
the key. The hippocampus in human infants is dramatically underdeveloped. Not slightly behind. Not almost there.

30
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:48,000
dramatically functionally incomplete in the first years of life. The neurons are there, but the connections between them, the dense complex network of links that

31
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:56,000
allows efficient memory encoding and retrieval, hasn't formed yet. The hippocampus undergo significant development through the first 2 to 3

32
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:04,000
years of life and in some regions continues developing well into adolescence. A baby experiencing something beautiful or frightening or

33
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:12,000
joyful is like trying to record a video on a camera that doesn't have a memory card yet. The lens is working. The processor is running, but there's

34
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:16,000
nowhere for the footage to go. So, the memories don't form in the first place.

35
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:24,000
Not in the retrievable narrative sense, not the kind you can pull up 30 years later and play back. That's the first part of the answer. But it's not the

36
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:32,000
whole answer because it still doesn't explain why most people can't access memories from age 2 or three, even though the hippocampus is considerably

37
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:39,000
more developed by then than it was at birth. This is where it gets even more interesting. There's a second factor and it has to do with something called

38
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:48,000
neurogenesis. Your brain grows new neurons throughout your life. This was considered controversial for a long time. The old assumption was that you

39
00:04:48,000 --> 00:05:00,000
were born with all the neurons you'd ever have and that was it. That turned out to be wrong. New neurons are generated continuously, particularly in the hippocampus. Now, here's the twist.

40
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:09,000
In early childhood, the rate of neurogenesis in the hippocampus is extraordinarily high, far higher than at any later point in life. The infant

41
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:18,000
brain is generating new hippocample neurons at a rate that simply doesn't happen in adults. And all of those new neurons need to be integrated into the

42
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:25,000
existing network. They need to find their place, form connections, wire themselves into the system. That

43
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:33,000
integration process is disruptive. It destabilizes existing connections. It essentially rewires the filing system.

44
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:41,000
Think of it like this. Imagine you have a library, not a big organized library, a young chaotic one that's adding

45
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:49,000
hundreds of new shelves every single day. Books are being added faster than they can be properly cataloged. The organization system keeps changing as

46
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:57,000
new sections are built. Old books don't necessarily stay where you put them because the shelves themselves keep moving. That's your hippo campus in the

47
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:06,000
first few years of life. The very thing that makes the infant brain so extraordinarily good at learning, that explosive growth, that relentless

48
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:15,000
generation of new neurons is simultaneously the thing that makes it almost impossible to form stable long-term memories.

49
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:24,000
Rapid neurogenesis trades away memory consolidation in exchange for learning capacity. The infant brain is optimized for absorbing new information at maximum

50
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:28,000
speed, not for filing it away in a form that will be accessible decades later.

51
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:35,000
You were learning faster than any adult ever could. And the cost of that speed was forgetting everything.

52
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:40,000
But there's a third factor, and this one is the strangest one of all. Language.

53
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:48,000
This sounds simple at first. Of course, babies can't form verbal memories. They don't have words yet. But it goes deeper than that. Because the relationship

54
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:52,000
between language and memory is more intimate than most people realize.

55
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:59,000
Autobiographical memory, the I remember when I was five and this happened kind of memory, is deeply structurally

56
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:08,000
connected to language. Not just because you need words to describe memories to other people, but because the internal experience of having a memory, of

57
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:16,000
replaying it in your mind, of knowing that it happened to you and not someone else, that experience is mediated by language in ways that are hard to fully

58
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:25,000
separate. When you remember something, you are in part narrating it to yourself. You're constructing a story, a

59
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:34,000
sequence, a sense of self moving through time. And that narrative capacity, that ability to construct a story with yourself as the protagonist emerges

60
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:40,000
alongside language. Infants have a sense of self. Research has demonstrated that.

61
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:48,000
But it's a present tense self and I am experiencing this right now self. The extension of that self backward through

62
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:56,000
time into a retrievable personal history, the autobiographical self requires language as scaffolding.

63
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:01,000
Without language, there's no story. Without a story, there's no narrator.

64
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:07,000
And without a narrator, there's no one to be the subject of the memory. You can't remember I was there if you don't

65
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:16,000
yet have a stable language mediated concept of I. Studies across cultures support this.

66
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:24,000
Children who develop language later also tend to have later onset of autobiographical memory. Bilingual children often have the clearest early

67
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:32,000
memories in the language they were using at the time, even if they rarely use that language as adults. People who learned sign language as their first

68
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:41,000
language show the same infantile amnesia patterns as hearing children who learned spoken language, suggesting that it's not spoken language specifically, but

69
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:49,000
language as a cognitive system that matters. The words aren't just how you express the memory. They're part of how the memory is built in the first place.

70
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:56,000
So now we have three overlapping explanations. Hippocample immaturity, explosive neurogenesis disrupting

71
00:08:56,000 --> 00:09:04,000
consolidation and the absence of language as a structuring scaffold for autobiographical experience. All three

72
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:11,000
are real. All three are happening simultaneously and together they create a window of near total amnesia that

73
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:18,000
covers the most formative period of your life. But here's what makes this genuinely strange. The experiences you

74
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:28,000
can't remember still shaped you. This is the part that people don't always fully sit with. Your infant years are gone from your conscious retrievable memory.

75
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:35,000
But they are absolutely not gone from you. Everything that happened in those years left traces. Deep structural

76
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:44,000
traces that influence who you are right now. The attachment patterns you formed with your caregivers in the first year of life. Whether you learned that the

77
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:51,000
world was safe and responsive or unpredictable and unreliable are encoded not as memories but as implicit

78
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:59,000
expectations, ways of being, emotional reflexes that operate below conscious awareness. If you were consistently

79
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:06,000
soothed when you cried, your nervous system learned at a biological level that distress is temporary and help is

80
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:15,000
available. That expectation shapes how you respond to stress as an adult, how quickly you can calm yourself down, how much you trust that things will be okay.

81
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:25,000
If you were inconsistently responded to or left to cry without comfort, your nervous system learned something different, not as a memory, as a

82
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:32,000
setting, a default calibration of how safe the world is and how much you can rely on other people. None of this is accessible to you as a story you can

83
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:39,000
tell. You can't remember the specific night you cried and no one came or the specific morning you were held and felt

84
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:48,000
completely safe, but those nights and mornings added up into something. A posture toward existence that you carry without knowing you're carrying it. This

85
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:56,000
is what psychologists mean when they talk about implicit memory as opposed to explicit memory. Explicit memory is the kind you can consciously access and

86
00:10:56,000 --> 00:11:07,000
describe. Implicit memory is the kind that lives in your body, your emotional reactions, your automatic assumptions about how relationships work, and whether you deserve to be cared for.

87
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:16,000
Infantile amnesia erases your explicit memories. It leaves your implicit memories completely intact. You forgot the experiences. You kept the lessons.

88
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:23,000
The problem is, you can't examine lessons you don't know you learned. You just act on them and wonder why you respond to certain situations the way

89
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:32,000
you do. Now, let's talk about the fake ones. Because here's where it gets uncomfortable. Some of the earliest memories you think you have are probably

90
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:40,000
not real, not distorted, not exaggerated, actually fabricated, constructed by your brain from photographs, stories, and repeated

91
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:48,000
tellings, and then filed as if they were genuine experiential memories. This is extremely well documented in memory

92
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:52,000
research, and it's deeply unsettling the first time you really confront it.

93
00:11:52,000 --> 00:12:00,000
Here's how it happens. Your family has a photo of you at your second birthday party. You've seen that photo dozens of times growing up. Adults told you the

94
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:08,000
story of that birthday. What happened, who was there, how you reacted to the cake. You heard that story enough times that your brain eventually built a

95
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:16,000
mental representation of the event. And then at some point, your brain filed that mental representation in the same place as actual memories. Now it feels

96
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:24,000
like a memory. It has the quality of a memory. You'd describe it as a memory if someone asked, but what you're actually accessing is a reconstruction built from

97
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:30,000
external information, not a genuine trace of the original experience.

98
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:38,000
Researchers have demonstrated that entirely false memories of events that never happened at all can be implanted in people through repeated suggestion

99
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:47,000
and social reinforcement. And those false memories are experientially indistinguishable from real ones. If that's possible with fabricated events,

100
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:54,000
the capacity for genuine experiences to be reconstructed and misfiled as direct memories is even more plausible. Think

101
00:12:54,000 --> 00:13:02,000
about the earliest memory you have right now, the one you'd reach for if someone asked, "Where did it come from? Did you

102
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:07,000
remember it always unprompted, or did someone tell you about it first?

103
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:18,000
Is there a photo, a family story that gets a family told at reunions?" The honest answer for most people is that they can't be sure.

104
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:25,000
And that uncertainty is not comfortable, but it's important. Your memory of your own earliest life is at least partly a

105
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:34,000
story other people told you about yourself that you eventually absorbed as your own. Which raises a strange philosophical question. If the memory is

106
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:44,000
vivid and emotionally real and functionally identical to a genuine memory in every way you can detect, does it matter that it might be a reconstruction?

107
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:53,000
Some people say no. The memory serves the same function regardless of its origin. Others find it deeply disorienting because the self you think

108
00:13:53,000 --> 00:14:02,000
you are is partly built on stories about your own past. And if those stories are borrowed rather than remembered, what does that mean for who you actually are?

109
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:09,000
There's no clean answer to that, but it's worth sitting with. Let's come back to something more concrete. Why do some people have earlier memories than

110
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:18,000
others? Because they do. The research consistently shows variation. Some people have what feel like genuine memories from as early as age 2. Others

111
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:26,000
have nothing before 5 or 6. The average onset of reliable autobiographical memory is around 3 to 3 and 1/2 years.

112
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:34,000
But the range is wide. Several factors seem to influence this. Culture is one of them. Studies comparing American

113
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:42,000
children with children from cultures where parents less frequently engage in collaborative reminiscing, talking with children about shared past experiences,

114
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:49,000
asking them questions about what they remember, narrating their own histories back to them, show that the cultural practice of memory talk significantly

115
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:54,000
affects how early children begin forming retrievable autobiographical memories.

116
00:14:54,000 --> 00:15:01,000
In cultures where parents frequently say things like, "Remember when we went to the beach last summer? What did you see?

117
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:10,000
Where memory is actively rehearsed and socially reinforced, children develop earlier and more detailed autobiographical memories. The social

118
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:21,000
practice of remembering together teaches children that their experiences are worth preserving and gives them the narrative scaffolding to do so. Memory, it turns out, is partly a social skill.

119
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:27,000
And like all social skills, it develops faster when it's actively practiced and encouraged.

120
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:35,000
Gender is another factor, though the reasons are complex. Studies have found that on average, women report earlier and more emotionally detailed childhood

121
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:46,000
memories than men. Whether this is biological or a product of different socialization patterns around emotional expression and narrative or some combination of both is still debated.

122
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:55,000
And then there's stress. Significant emotional experiences, particularly frightening ones, sometimes leave traces that other memories don't.

123
00:15:55,000 --> 00:16:02,000
The amygdala, the brain's emotional alarm system, is active and functional much earlier in development than the

124
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:10,000
hippo campus. Fear responses, intense emotional moments, can sometimes leave residues that surface later as feelings

125
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:19,000
or physical reactions without any accompanying narrative memory. You might become inexplicably anxious in a hospital or feel an unaccountable sense

126
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:24,000
of safety at the smell of a specific food without knowing why.

127
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:29,000
The emotion remembered the experience even when the story didn't.

128
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:37,000
Here's something that doesn't get discussed nearly enough in all of this. Sleep. Memory consolidation.

129
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:44,000
The process by which new experiences are converted into stable long-term memories happens primarily during sleep.

130
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:53,000
Specifically during slowwave sleep and REM sleep when the brain essentially replays and reorganizes the day's experiences, strengthening important

131
00:16:53,000 --> 00:17:02,000
connections and pruning unnecessary ones. Infants sleep enormously. A newborn sleeps 16 to 18 hours a day, and

132
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:11,000
their sleep architecture is radically different from adult sleep. They spend far more of their sleep time in active REMlike states than adults do. For

133
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:21,000
decades, researchers assumed this was just a biological quirk of development with no particular significance for memory. It turns out that might be wrong.

134
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:30,000
Some researchers now believe that the enormous amounts of active sleep in infancy serve a specific developmental function. Not storing discrete memories,

135
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:38,000
but consolidating the foundational patterns that underly learning. Learning how faces work, how voices work, how

136
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:46,000
physical objects behave in space, how social interaction flows. The infant sleeping for 18 hours is not wasting

137
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:53,000
time. The infant sleeping for 18 hours is building the operating system that everything else will run on, not saving

138
00:17:53,000 --> 00:18:01,000
individual files, installing the software. And one implication of this is that the experiences happening during those awake hours aren't being stored as

139
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:05,000
memories precisely because they're being used for something more fundamental.

140
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:14,000
They're being processed into architecture rather than narrative, into capability rather than story. You don't remember learning to see, but you can

141
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:21,000
see. You don't remember learning that objects are solid and permanent, but you know it instantly and effortlessly every

142
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:29,000
moment of your life. You don't remember learning to read faces and voices for emotional meaning, but you do it automatically thousands of times a day

143
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:37,000
without any conscious effort. All of that was learned in the years you can't remember. It just got filed in a different format than the memories you

144
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:44,000
can access. Deeper, more permanent, more fundamental. So, what does all of this mean for you right now? It means that

145
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:52,000
the first years of your life were not wasted just because you can't remember them. They were in many ways the most consequential years of your entire

146
00:18:52,000 --> 00:19:02,000
existence. The years when the foundational architecture of who you are was laid down at a level deeper than memory can reach. The person you are,

147
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:09,000
the way you attach to people, the way you respond to uncertainty, the things that calm you, and the things that

148
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:17,000
destabilize you, the intuitions you can't explain, and the fears that seem to come from nowhere.

149
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:26,000
All of that has roots in a period of your life that is completely invisible to your conscious mind. You are built from experiences you cannot access.

150
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:34,000
That's either terrifying or liberating depending on how you hold it. On the terrifying side, you can't examine the

151
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:41,000
foundations. You can't go back and audit the first three years to understand why you are the way you are. The influences

152
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:48,000
are real. They're shaping you constantly and they're entirely outside your conscious reach. On the liberating side,

153
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:57,000
the absence of explicit memory means the absence of explicit narrative. You don't have a story you tell yourself about those years. And that means the story

154
00:19:57,000 --> 00:20:04,000
isn't fixed. The implicit patterns can be changed not by remembering because there's nothing to remember, but by new

155
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:11,000
experiences that update the old calibrations. therapy, safe relationships, consistent new evidence

156
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:19,000
that the world is different from what your three-month-old self concluded it was. The brain is plastic. The early

157
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:28,000
patterns are influential, but they're not destiny. They're just the starting position. And here's the last thing worth considering. Every generation is

158
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:36,000
shaped by experiences they can't remember, passed down from caregivers who were shaped by experiences they couldn't remember either. The implicit

159
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:43,000
emotional inheritance moves through families across generations. Anxiety patterns, attachment styles, default

160
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:50,000
expectations about whether the world is safe without anyone being able to point to the original source or consciously decide whether to pass it on. You

161
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:58,000
received something from your caregivers that came from their caregivers, not as stories, not as explicit teaching, as calibration, as the emotional atmosphere

162
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:06,000
you breathed in your earliest years before you had any awareness that you were absorbing it. The memories are gone, but what they built is sitting

163
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:15,000
inside you right now, quietly running the show in ways you barely notice. The first years of your life happened. They mattered more than almost any other

164
00:21:15,000 --> 00:21:18,000
years that followed. You just weren't allowed to keep the receipts.
